If it was your own bones that you had deliberately tried to leave for 
later use by a future, less-prepared character?  Yes; that's cheating.

> Suppose I had a very good game going and encountered a bones level, 
> somewhere around level 20? Where would you still consider it a major 
> advantage?

The existence of a bones level itself isn't necessarily a major
advantage; it has to do with the content of the bones.  If those bones
contain the Eye of Mumble, the Eyes of the Overworld, and a full-on
ascension kit, and my character happens to be Neutral, then it's
obviously a major advantage.  If they contain a cursed orcish helm and
some scrolls of punishment, noticeably less so.

That said, bones files are part of the game as currently written, and
the potential for their existence is certainly there.  Whether they 
_should_ be allowed to create such a huge swing in the game is a
completely different question from whether a player who's playing should
be willing to use those bones-files and their loot.  

I wouldn't be inclined to DQ such an ascension at all.  I also wouldn't 
necessarily be willing to say, though, that it showed that an 11-conduct 
ascension is possible _without_ bones; just that, with the game as 
written, 11 conducts certainly _is_ possible.  (This goes back to Shawn
Moore's proof-of-concept 12-conduct ascension for a similar example;
what it really shows is that playing straight vanilla and getting a
good, healthy stroke of luck, 12 conducts is possible.)

